@[system]

※※ JSON read out of bytes and written onto them.
※
※ Imported as `⎕import("json")`.  `decode` reads a document -- RFC 8259's, and
※ what `Extensions` adds to it -- into a `Value` made in an arena the caller
※ hands over, and `encode` writes a `Value` back as UTF-8, on one line or
※ pretty-printed over several, into an arena as well.  `equal` says whether two
※ values are one, and `finite` whether a value can be written as JSON.
※
※ Reading is simdjson's two stages, written in the language.  The first finds
※ every character that matters -- the brackets, the commas and colons, where a
※ string or another value begins -- sixty-four bytes at a time: the block is
※ compared with each class of byte at once, the comparisons are gathered into
※ the bits of a `u64` by `⎕mask`, and the strings are found by arithmetic on
※ those bits -- the quotes a backslash escapes taken out, and what lies between
※ two quotes by a prefix exclusive or.  The places are walked off the bits with
※ `⎕trail` into a list.  The second stage reads the values from those places in
※ one loop, with a stack of its own for what is open, so that how deep a
※ document goes is not how deep the machine's stack does.  A string is read
※ thirty-two bytes at a time to its first quote, backslash or control
※ character, and copied out in one move.
※
※ What is read is put in the arena the caller names, and only there: every
※ list and string of the document is made there, so a value goes into the list
※ holding it as it is, and the whole document is given back at once when the
※ arena is -- what simdjson's and yyjson's documents do with a pool of their own.
※ What is written is made in an arena too, the caller's to give back when it
※ likes.

unit ¤size → ¤idx

let string := ⎕import("string")

※ -- the types -----------------------------------------------------------------

※※ A JSON value: what a document is, and what each element and member of one is.
※※
※※ \details A number is kept as it was written where that can be done exactly:
※※ an `integer` where the text has no point and no exponent and the number fits
※※ an `i64`, `large` where it is above that and fits a `u64`, and a `number` --
※※ the `f64` nearest it -- otherwise.  An object is its members in the order the
※※ document wrote them, keys written twice among them unless `decode` was told
※※ otherwise.
@[export]
type Value = null : void | boolean : bool | integer : i64 | large : u64 | number : f64 | text : str | array : [Value] | object : [Member]

※※ One member of an object: a key and what it stands for.
@[export]
type Member = key : str ; value : Value

※※ What `decode` reads besides RFC 8259.
※※
※※ \details Flags, combined with `|`; `strict` is none of them and the default.
※※
※※ - `comments`: `//` to the end of the line and `/*` to `*/`, where white space
※※   may stand.
※※ - `hash_comments`: `#` to the end of the line, as YAML and many configuration
※※   formats have it.
※※ - `trailing_commas`: a comma after the last element of an array or member of
※※   an object.
※※ - `single_quotes`: a string between `'` and `'`, in which `"` stands for itself
※※   and `\'` for a `'`.
※※ - `unquoted_keys`: a key written as an identifier -- a letter, `_` or `$`, then
※※   letters, digits, `_` and `$`; a byte above ASCII counts as a letter, and the
※※   key is checked as UTF-8 as any string is.
※※ - `special_numbers`: `NaN`, `Infinity` and `-Infinity`, spelt so.
※※ - `hex_numbers`: `0x` and hexadecimal digits, a whole number.
※※ - `leading_plus`: a `+` in front of a number.
※※ - `loose_points`: a point with digits on one side of it only, `.5` and `5.`.
※※ - `byte_order_mark`: the three bytes `EF BB BF` in front of the document.
※※ - `control_characters`: a control character in a string as it is.
※※ - `several`: more than one value at the top level, one after another --
※※   JSON Lines, and documents simply concatenated; the answer is then an array of
※※   them, however many there are.
※※ - `lone_surrogates`: a `\u` escape of half a surrogate pair with no other half
※※   stands for U+FFFD, the replacement character, rather than being refused.
※※ - `escapes`: JSON5's further escapes -- `\'`, `\v`, `\0`, `\x` and two digits,
※※   a backslash before a line's end standing for nothing, and any other
※※   character after a backslash for itself.
※※ - `whitespace`: JSON5's further white space -- vertical tab, form feed, U+00A0,
※※   U+FEFF, U+2028, U+2029 and the spaces of Unicode's `Zs`.
※※ - `json5`: all that JSON5 adds: `comments`, `trailing_commas`, `single_quotes`,
※※   `unquoted_keys`, `special_numbers`, `hex_numbers`, `leading_plus`,
※※   `loose_points`, `escapes` and `whitespace`.
@[export, flag]
enum Extensions : u32:
    strict = 0 ;
    comments = 1 ;
    hash_comments = 2 ;
    trailing_commas = 4 ;
    single_quotes = 8 ;
    unquoted_keys = 16 ;
    special_numbers = 32 ;
    hex_numbers = 64 ;
    leading_plus = 128 ;
    loose_points = 256 ;
    byte_order_mark = 512 ;
    control_characters = 1024 ;
    several = 2048 ;
    lone_surrogates = 4096 ;
    escapes = 8192 ;
    whitespace = 16384 ;
    json5 = 25085

※※ What `decode` does with a key an object has already had.
※※
※※ \details `keep` keeps every member as written, which is the default and what
※※ a list of members can hold; `first` keeps the first and drops the rest;
※※ `last` keeps the first one's place with the last one's value, which is what
※※ Python and JavaScript do; `refuse` refuses the document.
@[export]
enum Duplicates:
    keep ;
    first ;
    last ;
    refuse

※※ Why a document was refused.
※※
※※ \details
※※ - `truncated`: the bytes ended where more of the document was wanted.
※※ - `unexpected`: a byte stands where nothing it could begin or end may.
※※ - `malformed_number`: a number begun and broken off, or written as JSON does
※※   not write one -- a nought in front of another digit, a point with no digits
※※   after it.
※※ - `out_of_range`: a number beyond the largest `f64`.
※※ - `bad_escape`: a backslash and something that is not an escape.
※※ - `lone_surrogate`: a `\u` escape of half a surrogate pair, the other half not
※※   after it.
※※ - `control_character`: a byte below U+0020 in a string, unescaped.
※※ - `not_utf8`: bytes in a string that are not UTF-8.
※※ - `trailing`: something after the value, where only white space may be.
※※ - `too_deep`: arrays and objects nested deeper than `decode` was told to go.
※※ - `duplicate_key`: a key an object already had, under `Duplicates.refuse`.
※※ - `too_large`: a document of four gigabytes or more, whose places do not fit
※※   the 32 bits they are kept in.
@[export]
enum Problem:
    truncated ;
    unexpected ;
    malformed_number ;
    out_of_range ;
    bad_escape ;
    lone_surrogate ;
    control_character ;
    not_utf8 ;
    trailing ;
    too_deep ;
    duplicate_key ;
    too_large

※※ A document refused: why, and where.
※※
※※ \details `at` is the offset of the byte the problem was found at, from the
※※ first byte of the input; `line` and `column` say the same counted from one,
※※ a line ending at each line feed and a column being a character, as an
※※ editor shows it.
@[export]
type Error = problem : Problem ; at : u64 ¤idx ; line : u64 ; column : u64

※ What the second stage is told, once for the whole document.
type How = allow : Extensions ; duplicates : Duplicates

※ A value read, and the place in the list of structural characters after it.
type Read = value : Value ; next : u64 ¤idx

※ A number or a literal read, and the place of the byte after it.
type Scalar = value : Value ; end : u64 ¤idx

※ -- bytes ---------------------------------------------------------------------

※ Whether `flag` is among `allow`.
@[inline]
fn allows(allow: Extensions, flag: Extensions) → bool:
    (allow & flag) = flag

※ Whether a byte is the white space RFC 8259 has: space, tab, line feed and
※ carriage return.
@[inline]
fn is_space(c: u8) → bool:
    c = 32u8 or c = 10u8 or c = 13u8 or c = 9u8

※ Whether a byte is one of the six structural characters: `{` and `[` differ by
※ one bit, as do `}` and `]`, so one `|` asks about each pair.
@[inline]
fn is_operator(c: u8) → bool:
    let folded: u8 = c | 32u8
    folded = 123u8 or folded = 125u8 or c = 58u8 or c = 44u8

※ How many bytes of JSON5's further white space begin at `at`: nought where
※ none does.
fn more_space(input: u8⟦⟧, at: u64 ¤idx) → u64 ¤idx:
    let n: u64 ¤idx = #input
    let c: u8 = input⟦at⟧
    if c = 11u8 or c = 12u8:
        return 1
    if c = 0xC2u8 and at + 1 < n and input⟦at + 1⟧ = 0xA0u8:
        return 2
    if at + 2 ≥ n:
        return 0
    let second: u8 = input⟦at + 1⟧
    let third: u8 = input⟦at + 2⟧
    ※ U+FEFF, U+1680, U+2000 to U+200A, U+2028, U+2029, U+202F, U+205F, U+3000
    if c = 0xEFu8 and second = 0xBBu8 and third = 0xBFu8:
        return 3
    if c = 0xE1u8 and second = 0x9Au8 and third = 0x80u8:
        return 3
    if c = 0xE2u8 and second = 0x80u8 and (third ≤ 0x8Au8 or third = 0xA8u8 or third = 0xA9u8
                                           or third = 0xAFu8):
        return 3
    if c = 0xE2u8 and second = 0x81u8 and third = 0x9Fu8:
        return 3
    if c = 0xE3u8 and second = 0x80u8 and third = 0x80u8:
        return 3
    0

※ How many bytes of white space or a comment begin at `at`, as `allow` has them
※ besides RFC 8259's four: nought where none does.  A comment not closed runs
※ to the end.
fn skipped(input: u8⟦⟧, at: u64 ¤idx, allow: Extensions) → u64 ¤idx:
    let n: u64 ¤idx = #input
    let c: u8 = input⟦at⟧
    if is_space(c):
        return 1
    if c = 47u8 and allows(allow, Extensions.comments) and at + 1 < n:
        let next: u8 = input⟦at + 1⟧
        if next = 47u8:
            let end: mut u64 ¤idx = at + 2
            while §line end < n and input⟦end⟧ ≠ 10u8:
                end ← end + 1
            return end - at
        if next = 42u8:
            let end: mut u64 ¤idx = at + 2
            while §block end + 1 < n and ¬(input⟦end⟧ = 42u8 and input⟦end + 1⟧ = 47u8):
                end ← end + 1
            return if end + 1 < n: end + 2 - at else: n - at
    if c = 35u8 and allows(allow, Extensions.hash_comments):
        let end: mut u64 ¤idx = at + 1
        while §hash end < n and input⟦end⟧ ≠ 10u8:
            end ← end + 1
        return end - at
    if allows(allow, Extensions.whitespace):
        return more_space(input, at)
    0

※ -- the first stage -----------------------------------------------------------
※
※ What it answers is the place of every byte the second stage begins something
※ at: the six structural characters, the quote opening a string, and the first
※ byte of every other run of bytes that is neither white space nor structural --
※ a number, a literal, a key without quotes, and anything that is none of them
※ and will be refused where it stands.  A byte inside a string is none of these.
※ The last place is the length of the input, so that the second stage finds the
※ end the way it finds anything else.

※ The bits of the 64-bit arithmetic: every odd bit, and every bit.
let ODD_BITS: u64 = 0xAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA
let ALL_BITS: u64 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF

※ What one block hands the next: whether its first byte is escaped, whether it
※ begins inside a string (every bit, or none), whether the byte before it was
※ part of a run that is not a string -- and, of this block, the bits of the
※ bytes something begins at.
type Carry = escaped : u64 ; inside : u64 ; scalar : u64 ; starts : u64

※ The bytes something begins at in a block of sixty-four, and what the block
※ after it is to be told.
※
※ **Escaped**: a byte after an odd run of backslashes.  Backslashes that begin a
※ run on an even place, minus that run, carry into the byte after it where the
※ run is odd -- which subtraction does for every run at once, the odd places
※ brought in first and taken out after (simdjson's `json_escape_scanner`).
※ **In a string**: a quote not escaped flips it, which is a prefix exclusive or
※ of the quotes -- the lower half of their carry-less product with all ones,
※ one instruction where the machine has it and the ladder `x ^= x « 1`, `« 2`,
※ and so on to `« 32` where it has not (simdjson's `prefix_xor`) -- continued
※ from the block before.  **Begins something**: a structural
※ character, or a byte that is neither white space nor structural after one
※ that is either -- a quote counted as the end of a run, so that what follows
※ the end of a string begins again -- and not inside a string, the quote that
※ opens one excepted.
@[inline]
fn block_starts(block: u8⟦64⟧, before: Carry) → Carry:
    let quote: u64 = ⎕mask(block = 34u8)
    let backslash: u64 = ⎕mask(block = 92u8)
    let folded: u8⟦64⟧ = block | 32u8
    let operator: u64 = ⎕mask((folded = 123u8) ∨ (folded = 125u8) ∨ (block = 58u8) ∨ (block = 44u8))
    let space: u64 = ⎕mask((block = 32u8) ∨ (block = 10u8) ∨ (block = 13u8) ∨ (block = 9u8))
    let escaped: mut u64 = before.escaped
    let carried: mut u64 = 0u64
    if backslash ≠ 0u64:
        let potential: u64 = backslash & ~before.escaped
        let codes: u64 = ⎕wrap(((potential « 1u64) | ODD_BITS) - potential) ^ ODD_BITS
        escaped ← codes ^ (backslash | before.escaped)
        carried ← (codes & backslash) » 63u64
    let real: u64 = quote & ~escaped
    let in_string: u64 = ⎕clmul(real, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFu64)⟦1⟧ ^ before.inside
    let scalar: u64 = ~(operator | space)
    let plain: u64 = scalar & ~real
    let follows: u64 = (plain « 1u64) | before.scalar
    let begins: u64 = scalar & ~follows
    let tail: u64 = in_string ^ real
    Carry(.escaped ← carried, .inside ← ⎕wrap(0u64 - (in_string » 63u64)),
          .scalar ← plain » 63u64, .starts ← (operator | begins) & ~tail)

※ Where everything begins in `input`, from `start` on, sixty-four bytes at a
※ time: RFC 8259's way of writing strings and white space, which is every
※ extension's but the four `slowly` reads.  Each block is copied into room of
※ its own -- the last one, short, into room of spaces, which begin nothing, so
※ that it is read as the others are -- and its bits walked off with `⎕trail`,
※ the lowest first, into room of the frame's, which is joined onto `found` in
※ one move: a join is a call or three, a place written in the frame is not.  The places are made in `s`, an arena `decode`
※ gives back when it is done with them.
fn quickly(input: u8⟦⟧, start: u64 ¤idx, s: &mut arena) → [u32] in s:
    let n: u64 ¤idx = #input
    let found: mut [u32] = [] in s
    ⎕reserve(found, (n - start) ÷ 4 ?? 0)
    let carry: mut Carry = Carry(.escaped ← 0u64, .inside ← 0u64, .scalar ← 0u64, .starts ← 0u64)
    let places: mut u32⟦64⟧ = 64 ⍴ 0u32
    let at: mut u64 ¤idx = start
    while §blocks at < n:
        if at + 64 ≤ n:
            let block: u8⟦64⟧ = input⟦at…at + 64⟧
            carry ← block_starts(block, carry)
        else:
            let last: mut u8⟦64⟧ = 64 ⍴ 32u8
            last⟦0…n - at⟧ ← input⟦at…n⟧
            carry ← block_starts(last, carry)
        ※ the block's places into room of the frame's, and joined on in one move
        let base: u32 = ⎕narrow(⎕drop(at), ⌜u32⌝) ?? 0u32
        let bits: mut u64 = carry.starts
        let count: mut u64 ¤idx = 0
        while §bits bits ≠ 0u64:
            places⟦count⟧ ← base + ⎕widen(⎕trail(bits), ⌜u32⌝)
            count ← count + 1
            bits ← bits & ⎕wrap(bits - 1u64)
        found ⧺← places⟦0…count⟧
        at ← at + 64
    found ⧺← ⟦⎕narrow(⎕drop(n), ⌜u32⌝) ?? 0u32⟧
    found

※ Where everything begins in `input`, from `start` on, a byte at a time: what
※ `quickly` answers, for the extensions that change where a string or white
※ space is -- comments of either kind, single quotes and JSON5's white space.
※ The same rules otherwise, a backslash outside a string escaping the byte
※ after it as it does inside one.
fn slowly(input: u8⟦⟧, start: u64 ¤idx, allow: Extensions, s: &mut arena) → [u32] in s:
    let n: u64 ¤idx = #input
    let single: bool = allows(allow, Extensions.single_quotes)
    let found: mut [u32] = [] in s
    let at: mut u64 ¤idx = start
    ※ whether the byte before was part of a run that is not a string, and
    ※ whether it was a backslash escaping this one
    let in_run: mut bool = false
    let escaped: mut bool = false
    while §bytes at < n:
        let c: u8 = input⟦at⟧
        let gap: u64 ¤idx = if escaped: 0 else: skipped(input, at, allow)
        if gap ≠ 0:
            in_run ← false
            at ← at + gap
        elif is_operator(c):
            found ⧺← ⟦⎕narrow(⎕drop(at), ⌜u32⌝) ?? 0u32⟧
            in_run ← false
            escaped ← false
            at ← at + 1
        elif ¬escaped and (c = 34u8 or (c = 39u8 and single)):
            if ¬in_run:
                found ⧺← ⟦⎕narrow(⎕drop(at), ⌜u32⌝) ?? 0u32⟧
            ※ to the quote that closes it, a backslash taking the byte after it
            at ← at + 1
            while §string at < n and input⟦at⟧ ≠ c:
                at ← at + (if input⟦at⟧ = 92u8: 2 else: 1)
            at ← at + 1
            in_run ← false
        else:
            if ¬in_run:
                found ⧺← ⟦⎕narrow(⎕drop(at), ⌜u32⌝) ?? 0u32⟧
            in_run ← true
            escaped ← c = 92u8 and ¬escaped
            at ← at + 1
    found ⧺← ⟦⎕narrow(⎕drop(n), ⌜u32⌝) ?? 0u32⟧
    found

※ -- refusing ------------------------------------------------------------------

※ Why and where, as the second stage hands it up: two words, which travel in
※ registers.  The line and the column are worked out once, for the one problem
※ a document is refused with.
type Fault = problem : Problem ; at : u64 ¤idx

※ The error a fault is, with the line and the column it is on: a walk of the
※ bytes before it, a line ending at each line feed and a column being a
※ character -- every byte that does not continue a UTF-8 sequence.
fn failure(input: u8⟦⟧, fault: Fault) → Error:
    let line: mut u64 = 1u64
    let column: mut u64 = 1u64
    let i: mut u64 ¤idx = 0
    let stop: u64 ¤idx = fault.at ⌊ ⎕unit(⎕drop(#input), ⌜idx⌝)
    while §bytes i < stop:
        let c: u8 = input⟦i⟧
        if c = 10u8:
            line ← line + 1u64
            column ← 1u64
        elif c & 0xC0u8 ≠ 0x80u8:
            column ← column + 1u64
        i ← i + 1
    Error(.problem ← fault.problem, .at ← fault.at, .line ← line, .column ← column)

※ -- the second stage ----------------------------------------------------------
※
※ A value is read from the place of its first byte among those the first stage
※ found, and answers the place after its last.  Arrays and objects are read
※ without recursion, as simdjson's second stage reads them: what is open is a
※ stack of its own, so how deep a document goes costs a record on that stack
※ and not a frame of the machine's -- `depth` is a policy, not what keeps the
※ program on its stack.  What has been read and not yet put in an array or an
※ object is a list of its own, `pending`, which is reused as the document is
※ read; an array or an object, once closed, is made of what is on the top of it
※ in one list exactly as long as it has to be, and what it was made of is room
※ for what comes next.

※ The place of the `k`-th thing the first stage found.
@[inline]
fn place_of(index: u32⟦⟧, k: u64 ¤idx) → u64 ¤idx:
    ⎕unit(⎕widen(index⟦k⟧, ⌜u64⌝), ⌜idx⌝)

※ The byte at `at`, and nought at the end of the input -- which is never a
※ place a value begins at, a nought byte outside a string being refused where
※ it stands.
@[inline]
fn byte_at(input: u8⟦⟧, at: u64 ¤idx) → u8:
    if at < ⎕unit(⎕drop(#input), ⌜idx⌝): input⟦at⟧ else: 0u8

※ A byte at `at` that cannot stand there: `truncated` where it is the end.
fn misplaced(input: u8⟦⟧, at: u64 ¤idx) → Fault:
    let n: u64 ¤idx = #input
    Fault(.problem ← if at ≥ n: Problem.truncated else: Problem.unexpected, .at ← at)

※ Whether something that is not a string may end just before `at`: at the end,
※ before white space, a structural character or a comment.
fn ends_here(input: u8⟦⟧, at: u64 ¤idx, allow: Extensions) → bool:
    let n: u64 ¤idx = #input
    if at ≥ n:
        return true
    let c: u8 = input⟦at⟧
    is_space(c) or is_operator(c) or (allow ≠ Extensions.strict and skipped(input, at, allow) ≠ 0)

※ Whether a byte opens an array or an object.
@[inline]
fn opens(c: u8) → bool:
    (c | 32u8) = 123u8

※ A value that is neither an array nor an object, at the `k`-th place.
※
※ Kept a call of its own: put where `tree_at` calls it, its strings and numbers
※ would make that frame larger than RISC-V reaches with an offset.
@[inline(never)]
fn leaf_at(input: u8⟦⟧, index: u32⟦⟧, k: u64 ¤idx, a: &mut arena, how: How) → Value ? Fault in a:
    let at: u64 ¤idx = place_of(index, k)
    let c: u8 = byte_at(input, at)
    if c = 34u8 or (c = 39u8 and allows(how.allow, Extensions.single_quotes)):
        return Value(.text ← string_at(input, at, a, how.allow)?)
    let one: Scalar = scalar_at(input, at, how.allow)?
    if ¬ends_here(input, one.end, how.allow):
        return ⊥ Fault(.problem ← Problem.unexpected, .at ← one.end)
    one.value

※ An array or an object open: where in `pending` its values begin, where in
※ the keys its keys do, and which of the two it is.
type Open = values : u64 ¤idx ; keys : u64 ¤idx ; object : bool

※ How many elements a list has, as a place among them.
@[inline]
fn held(l: [T']) → u64 ¤idx:
    ⎕unit(⎕drop(#l), ⌜idx⌝)

※ The key of a member at the `k`-th place, and the colon after it; the value
※ begins two places on.
fn member_at(input: u8⟦⟧, index: u32⟦⟧, k: u64 ¤idx, a: &mut arena, how: How) → str ? Fault in a:
    let key: str = key_at(input, place_of(index, k), a, how.allow)?
    let colon: u64 ¤idx = place_of(index, k + 1)
    if byte_at(input, colon) ≠ 58u8:
        return ⊥ misplaced(input, colon)
    key

※ The `count` values from `first` on, as a list exactly as long.
fn elements_of(pending: [Value] in a, first: u64 ¤idx, count: u64 ¤idx, a: &mut arena) → [Value] in a:
    let items: mut [Value] = [] in a
    ⎕reserve(items, count)
    let i: mut u64 ¤idx = 0
    while §items i < count:
        items ⧺← [pending⟦first + i⟧]
        i ← i + 1
    items

※ The `count` members of the object `open` says, its keys among `keys` and its
※ values among `pending`, as a list exactly as long.
fn members_of(keys: [str] in a, pending: [Value] in a, open: Open, count: u64 ¤idx,
              a: &mut arena) → [Member] in a:
    let members: mut [Member] = [] in a
    ⎕reserve(members, count)
    let i: mut u64 ¤idx = 0
    while §members i < count:
        members ⧺← [Member(.key ← keys⟦open.keys + i⟧, .value ← pending⟦open.values + i⟧)]
        i ← i + 1
    members

※ The value whose first byte is the `k`-th place, and the place after it.
※
※ One loop: a value is read where one begins, and after it what it ends is
※ closed -- an array or an object made of what is on top of `pending`, put
※ there in its stead -- until what comes next is a comma, and another value, or
※ nothing is open any more.  `live` is how much of `pending` is in use; above
※ it, elements already there are written over rather than joined on.
fn tree_at(input: u8⟦⟧, index: u32⟦⟧, start: u64 ¤idx, depth: u64, a: &mut arena,
           how: How) → Read ? Fault in a:
    let trailing: bool = allows(how.allow, Extensions.trailing_commas)
    let checked: bool = how.duplicates ≠ Duplicates.keep
    let pending: mut [Value] = [] in a
    let live: mut u64 ¤idx = 0
    let keys: mut [str] = [] in a
    let keys_live: mut u64 ¤idx = 0
    ※ where each key was, beside it, for a policy that refuses one written twice
    let spots: mut [u32] = [] in a
    let open: mut [Open] = [] in a
    let k: mut u64 ¤idx = start
    while §values true:
        let at: u64 ¤idx = place_of(index, k)
        let c: u8 = byte_at(input, at)
        if opens(c):
            if ⎕drop(#open) ≥ depth:
                return ⊥ Fault(.problem ← Problem.too_deep, .at ← at)
            if byte_at(input, place_of(index, k + 1)) ≠ c + 2u8:
                open ⧺← [Open(.values ← live, .keys ← keys_live, .object ← c = 123u8)]
                k ← k + 1
                if c = 123u8:
                    let key: str = member_at(input, index, k, a, how)?
                    if keys_live < held(keys):
                        keys⟦keys_live⟧ ← key
                        spots⟦keys_live⟧ ← index⟦k⟧
                    else:
                        keys ⧺← [key]
                        spots ⧺← ⟦index⟦k⟧⟧
                    keys_live ← keys_live + 1
                    k ← k + 2
                continue §values
        ※ a value that is not open: a leaf, or empty and closed where it opened
        let made: mut Value = if ¬opens(c):
            k ← k + 1
            leaf_at(input, index, k - 1, a, how)?
        else:
            k ← k + 2
            if c = 91u8: Value(.array ← [] in a) else: Value(.object ← [] in a)
        ※ the value read, on top of what is pending; and then what it ends
        while §closing true:
            if #open = 0:
                return Read(.value ← made, .next ← k)
            if live < held(pending):
                pending⟦live⟧ ← made
            else:
                pending ⧺← [made]
            live ← live + 1
            let top: Open = open⟦#open - 1⟧
            let closer: u8 = if top.object: 125u8 else: 93u8
            let after: u64 ¤idx = place_of(index, k)
            let next: u8 = byte_at(input, after)
            k ← k + 1
            if next = 44u8:
                if ¬(trailing and byte_at(input, place_of(index, k)) = closer):
                    if top.object:
                        let key: str = member_at(input, index, k, a, how)?
                        if keys_live < held(keys):
                            keys⟦keys_live⟧ ← key
                            spots⟦keys_live⟧ ← index⟦k⟧
                        else:
                            keys ⧺← [key]
                            spots ⧺← ⟦index⟦k⟧⟧
                        keys_live ← keys_live + 1
                        k ← k + 2
                    continue §values
                k ← k + 1
            elif next ≠ closer:
                return ⊥ misplaced(input, after)
            ※ closed: made of what is on top of `pending`, which is room again
            let count: u64 ¤idx = live - top.values
            if top.object:
                let members: [Member] = members_of(keys, pending, top, count, a)
                made ← if checked:
                    Value(.object ← settled(members, spots⟦top.keys…top.keys + count⟧, a, how.duplicates)?)
                else:
                    Value(.object ← members)
                keys_live ← top.keys
            else:
                made ← Value(.array ← elements_of(pending, top.values, count, a))
            live ← top.values
            †open⟦#open - 1⟧
    ※ never reached: every way out of the loop is a `return`
    ⊥ Fault(.problem ← Problem.truncated, .at ← place_of(index, k))

※ The members of an object a key written twice is settled in as `duplicates`
※ says: the first kept and the rest dropped, the first's place with the last's
※ value, or the document refused at the second.  `keys` is where each key was.
※
※ A table says which keys there were and where each went in what is kept.
fn settled(members: [Member] in a, keys: u32⟦⟧, a: &mut arena,
           duplicates: Duplicates) → [Member] ? Fault in a:
    let kept: mut [Member] = [] in a
    let seen: mut ⸨str: u64⸩ = ⸨⸩ in a
    let last: bool = duplicates = Duplicates.last
    let i: mut u64 ¤idx = 0
    let count: u64 ¤idx = #members
    while §members i < count:
        let key: str = members⟦i⟧.key
        match seen⸨key⸩:
            u64(earlier):
                if duplicates = Duplicates.refuse:
                    return ⊥ Fault(.problem ← Problem.duplicate_key, .at ← place_of(keys, i))
                if last:
                    kept⟦⎕unit(earlier, ⌜idx⌝)⟧.value ← members⟦i⟧.value
            ⊥:
                seen⸨key⸩ ← ⎕drop(#kept)
                kept ⧺← [members⟦i⟧]
        i ← i + 1
    kept

※ A key, at `at`: a string, or with `unquoted_keys` an identifier.
fn key_at(input: u8⟦⟧, at: u64 ¤idx, a: &mut arena, allow: Extensions) → str ? Fault in a:
    let c: u8 = byte_at(input, at)
    if c = 34u8 or (c = 39u8 and allows(allow, Extensions.single_quotes)):
        return string_at(input, at, a, allow)
    if ¬allows(allow, Extensions.unquoted_keys) or ¬is_identifier(c, true):
        return ⊥ misplaced(input, at)
    let n: u64 ¤idx = #input
    let end: mut u64 ¤idx = at + 1
    while §letters end < n and is_identifier(input⟦end⟧, false):
        end ← end + 1
    if ¬ends_here(input, end, allow):
        return ⊥ Fault(.problem ← Problem.unexpected, .at ← end)
    match ⎕text(input⟦at…end⟧) in a:
        str(key):
            key
        ⊥(bad):
            ⊥ Fault(.problem ← Problem.not_utf8, .at ← at + ⎕unit(bad, ⌜idx⌝))

※ Whether a byte may stand in an identifier, first or after: ASCII's letters,
※ `_`, `$`, any byte above ASCII, and after the first the digits.
fn is_identifier(c: u8, first: bool) → bool:
    let letter: u8 = ⎕wrap((c | 32u8) - 97u8)
    letter < 26u8 or c = 95u8 or c = 36u8 or c ≥ 0x80u8 or (¬first and ⎕wrap(c - 48u8) < 10u8)

※ -- strings -------------------------------------------------------------------

※ The string whose opening quote is at `at`, as text made in `a`.
※
※ Thirty-two bytes at a time, to the first byte that is a quote, a backslash or
※ a control character (`stop_at`): where that is the closing quote, the bytes
※ between are the string, checked and copied by `⎕text` in one move.  Where it
※ is a backslash, the rest is read by `escaped`, which goes from one of them to
※ the next the same way.
fn string_at(input: u8⟦⟧, at: u64 ¤idx, a: &mut arena, allow: Extensions) → str ? Fault in a:
    let n: u64 ¤idx = #input
    let quote: u8 = input⟦at⟧
    let begin: u64 ¤idx = at + 1
    let end: mut u64 ¤idx = begin
    let raw: bool = allows(allow, Extensions.control_characters)
    while §scan true:
        end ← stop_at(input, end, quote)
        if end ≥ n:
            return ⊥ Fault(.problem ← Problem.truncated, .at ← n)
        let c: u8 = input⟦end⟧
        if c = quote:
            break §scan
        if c = 92u8:
            return escaped(input, begin, end, a, allow)
        if ¬raw:
            return ⊥ Fault(.problem ← Problem.control_character, .at ← end)
        end ← end + 1
    match ⎕text(input⟦begin…end⟧) in a:
        str(text):
            text
        ⊥(bad):
            ⊥ Fault(.problem ← Problem.not_utf8, .at ← begin + ⎕unit(bad, ⌜idx⌝))

※ The place of the first quote, backslash or control character from `from` on,
※ or the end: thirty-two bytes at a time, the last block copied into room of
※ quotes, so that it is read as the others are and stops where the bytes do.
@[inline]
fn stop_at(input: u8⟦⟧, from: u64 ¤idx, quote: u8) → u64 ¤idx:
    let n: u64 ¤idx = #input
    let at: mut u64 ¤idx = from
    while §blocks true:
        let stops: mut u32 = 0u32
        if at + 32 ≤ n:
            let block: u8⟦32⟧ = input⟦at…at + 32⟧
            stops ← ⎕mask((block = quote) ∨ (block = 92u8) ∨ (block < 32u8))
        else:
            let last: mut u8⟦32⟧ = 32 ⍴ quote
            last⟦0…n - at⟧ ← input⟦at…n⟧
            stops ← ⎕mask((last = quote) ∨ (last = 92u8) ∨ (last < 32u8))
        if stops ≠ 0u32:
            return (at + ⎕unit(⎕widen(⎕trail(stops), ⌜u64⌝), ⌜idx⌝)) ⌊ n
        at ← at + 32
    n

※ The rest of a string with an escape in it: `begin` is its first byte and
※ `from` the first backslash.  What it stands for is gathered in a list -- the
※ runs between escapes in one move each -- and made text once it is all there.
※ The list is the heap's and given back on the way out, so that the arena holds
※ the text and not the bytes it was made from as well.
fn escaped(input: u8⟦⟧, begin: u64 ¤idx, from: u64 ¤idx, a: &mut arena,
           allow: Extensions) → str ? Fault in a:
    let n: u64 ¤idx = #input
    let quote: u8 = input⟦begin - 1⟧
    let raw: bool = allows(allow, Extensions.control_characters)
    let more: bool = allows(allow, Extensions.escapes)
    let out: mut [u8] = []
    out ⧺← input⟦begin…from⟧
    let at: mut u64 ¤idx = from
    while §runs true:
        if at ≥ n:
            return ⊥ Fault(.problem ← Problem.truncated, .at ← n)
        let c: u8 = input⟦at⟧
        if c = quote:
            break §runs
        if c = 92u8:
            if at + 1 ≥ n:
                return ⊥ Fault(.problem ← Problem.truncated, .at ← n)
            let e: u8 = input⟦at + 1⟧
            let simple: u8 = simple_escape(e, quote, more)
            if simple ≠ 0u8:
                out ⧺← ⟦simple⟧
                at ← at + 2
            elif e = 117u8:
                let code: Code = unicode_escape(input, at, allow)?
                out ← code_point(code.point, out)
                at ← code.end
            elif more:
                let skip: u64 ¤idx = json5_escape(input, at)
                if skip = 0:
                    return ⊥ Fault(.problem ← Problem.bad_escape, .at ← at)
                if e = 48u8:
                    out ⧺← ⟦0u8⟧
                elif e = 120u8:
                    let high: u8 = string.digit_value(input⟦at + 2⟧) ?? 0u8
                    let low: u8 = string.digit_value(input⟦at + 3⟧) ?? 0u8
                    out ← code_point(⎕widen(high × 16u8 + low, ⌜u32⌝), out)
                elif ¬line_end(input, at + 1):
                    ※ any other character stands for itself, all its bytes; a line's
                    ※ end after a backslash stands for nothing
                    out ⧺← input⟦at + 1…at + skip⟧
                at ← at + skip
            else:
                return ⊥ Fault(.problem ← Problem.bad_escape, .at ← at)
        elif c < 32u8 and ¬raw:
            return ⊥ Fault(.problem ← Problem.control_character, .at ← at)
        else:
            ※ a run of bytes that are none of the three, in one move
            let run: u64 ¤idx = stop_at(input, at + 1, quote)
            out ⧺← input⟦at…run⟧
            at ← run
    match ⎕text(out) in a:
        str(text):
            text
        ⊥(_):
            ⊥ Fault(.problem ← Problem.not_utf8, .at ← ill_formed(input, begin, at))

※ A code point written onto `into` as UTF-8, one byte to four.
fn code_point(n: u32, into: mut [u8]) → [u8]:
    if n < 0x80u32:
        into ⧺← ⟦low_byte(n)⟧
    elif n < 0x800u32:
        into ⧺← ⟦low_byte(0xC0u32 | (n » 6u32)), low_byte(0x80u32 | (n & 0x3Fu32))⟧
    elif n < 0x10000u32:
        into ⧺← ⟦low_byte(0xE0u32 | (n » 12u32)), low_byte(0x80u32 | ((n » 6u32) & 0x3Fu32)),
                 low_byte(0x80u32 | (n & 0x3Fu32))⟧
    else:
        into ⧺← ⟦low_byte(0xF0u32 | (n » 18u32)), low_byte(0x80u32 | ((n » 12u32) & 0x3Fu32)),
                 low_byte(0x80u32 | ((n » 6u32) & 0x3Fu32)), low_byte(0x80u32 | (n & 0x3Fu32))⟧
    into

※ The low byte of a number known to fit one.
fn low_byte(n: u32) → u8:
    ⎕narrow(n, ⌜u8⌝) ?? 0u8

※ What a one-character escape stands for, the character after the backslash
※ being `e`: nought where it is not one.  `\'` is one inside single quotes, and
※ with JSON5's escapes; `\v` with them.
fn simple_escape(e: u8, quote: u8, more: bool) → u8:
    if e = 34u8 or e = 92u8 or e = 47u8:
        e
    elif e = 110u8:
        10u8
    elif e = 116u8:
        9u8
    elif e = 114u8:
        13u8
    elif e = 98u8:
        8u8
    elif e = 102u8:
        12u8
    elif e = 39u8 and (quote = 39u8 or more):
        39u8
    elif e = 118u8 and more:
        11u8
    else:
        0u8

※ How many bytes one of JSON5's further escapes takes, from its backslash at
※ `at`: `\0` not before a digit, `\x` and two hexadecimal digits, a backslash
※ before a line feed, a carriage return (and a line feed after it), U+2028 or
※ U+2029 -- which stand for nothing -- and a backslash before any other
※ character but a digit, which stands for that character.  Nought where it is
※ none of them.
fn json5_escape(input: u8⟦⟧, at: u64 ¤idx) → u64 ¤idx:
    let n: u64 ¤idx = #input
    let e: u8 = input⟦at + 1⟧
    if e = 48u8:
        return if at + 2 < n and ⎕wrap(input⟦at + 2⟧ - 48u8) < 10u8: 0 else: 2
    if ⎕wrap(e - 49u8) < 9u8:
        return 0
    if e = 120u8:
        if (at + 3 < n and (string.digit_value(input⟦at + 2⟧) ?? 99u8) < 16u8
                and (string.digit_value(input⟦at + 3⟧) ?? 99u8) < 16u8):
            return 4
        return 0
    if e = 10u8:
        return 2
    if e = 13u8:
        return if at + 2 < n and input⟦at + 2⟧ = 10u8: 3 else: 2
    if e = 0xE2u8 and at + 3 < n and input⟦at + 2⟧ = 0x80u8 and (input⟦at + 3⟧ = 0xA8u8
                                                              or input⟦at + 3⟧ = 0xA9u8):
        return 4
    if e < 0x80u8:
        return 2
    ※ a character of more than one byte, which stands for itself whole -- as many
    ※ bytes as its leading one says, which `⎕text` checks with the rest
    let length: u64 ¤idx = if e ≥ 0xF0u8: 4 elif e ≥ 0xE0u8: 3 else: 2
    if at + 1 + length > n:
        return 0
    1 + length

※ Whether a line ends at `at`: a line feed, a carriage return, U+2028 or U+2029.
fn line_end(input: u8⟦⟧, at: u64 ¤idx) → bool:
    let c: u8 = input⟦at⟧
    (c = 10u8 or c = 13u8
        or (c = 0xE2u8 and at + 2 < ⎕unit(⎕drop(#input), ⌜idx⌝) and input⟦at + 1⟧ = 0x80u8
            and (input⟦at + 2⟧ = 0xA8u8 or input⟦at + 2⟧ = 0xA9u8)))

※ A code point a `\u` escape names, and the place after the escape.
type Code = point : u32 ; end : u64 ¤idx

※ The four hexadecimal digits at `at`, as a number: nothing where they are not
※ four of them.
fn four_digits(input: u8⟦⟧, at: u64 ¤idx) → u32?:
    if at + 4 > ⎕unit(⎕drop(#input), ⌜idx⌝):
        return ⊥
    let value: mut u32 = 0u32
    let i: mut u64 ¤idx = 0
    while §digits i < 4:
        let d: u8 = string.digit_value(input⟦at + i⟧) ?? 99u8
        if d ≥ 16u8:
            return ⊥
        value ← (value « 4u32) | ⎕widen(d, ⌜u32⌝)
        i ← i + 1
    value

※ The code point of the `\u` escape whose backslash is at `at`: a pair of them
※ where the first is the high half of a surrogate pair and the second the low,
※ and U+FFFD for half a pair alone where `lone_surrogates` says so.
fn unicode_escape(input: u8⟦⟧, at: u64 ¤idx, allow: Extensions) → Code ? Fault:
    let first: u32 = four_digits(input, at + 2) ?? 0x110000u32
    if first = 0x110000u32:
        return ⊥ Fault(.problem ← Problem.bad_escape, .at ← at)
    if first < 0xD800u32 or first > 0xDFFFu32:
        return Code(.point ← first, .end ← at + 6)
    let lone: bool = allows(allow, Extensions.lone_surrogates)
    if first ≤ 0xDBFFu32 and byte_at(input, at + 6) = 92u8 and byte_at(input, at + 7) = 117u8:
        let second: u32 = four_digits(input, at + 8) ?? 0x110000u32
        if second = 0x110000u32:
            return ⊥ Fault(.problem ← Problem.bad_escape, .at ← at + 6)
        if second ≥ 0xDC00u32 and second ≤ 0xDFFFu32:
            return Code(.point ← 0x10000u32 + ((first - 0xD800u32) « 10u32) + (second - 0xDC00u32),
                        .end ← at + 12)
    if lone:
        return Code(.point ← 0xFFFDu32, .end ← at + 6)
    ⊥ Fault(.problem ← Problem.lone_surrogate, .at ← at)

※ Where the first byte that is not UTF-8 is in a string with escapes in it,
※ from `begin` to `end`: the escapes stepped over, what is between read a
※ character at a time.  Asked only once the string is known to hold one.
fn ill_formed(input: u8⟦⟧, begin: u64 ¤idx, end: u64 ¤idx) → u64 ¤idx:
    let at: mut u64 ¤idx = begin
    while §characters at < end:
        let c: u8 = input⟦at⟧
        if c = 92u8:
            at ← at + 2
        elif c < 0x80u8:
            at ← at + 1
        else:
            match string.decode_char(input⟦at…end⟧):
                string.Parsed(char)(p):
                    at ← at + p.end
                ⊥:
                    return at
    ※ only an escape of JSON5's, a character after a backslash, is left
    begin

※ -- numbers and literals ------------------------------------------------------

※ What `allow` asks of `std.string`'s readers.
fn number_allow(allow: Extensions) → string.Allow:
    let found: mut string.Allow = string.Allow.json
    if allows(allow, Extensions.leading_plus):
        found ← found | string.Allow.plus
    if allows(allow, Extensions.hex_numbers):
        found ← found | string.Allow.hex
    if allows(allow, Extensions.loose_points):
        found ← found | string.Allow.loose
    found

※ Whether the bytes at `at` are `word`.
fn spelled(input: u8⟦⟧, at: u64 ¤idx, word: u8⟦⟧) → bool:
    let length: u64 ¤idx = #word
    if at + length > ⎕unit(⎕drop(#input), ⌜idx⌝):
        return false
    let i: mut u64 ¤idx = 0
    while §letters i < length:
        if input⟦at + i⟧ ≠ word⟦i⟧:
            return false
        i ← i + 1
    true

※ A literal or a number at `at`, and where it ends.
fn scalar_at(input: u8⟦⟧, at: u64 ¤idx, allow: Extensions) → Scalar ? Fault in ⎕static:
    let c: u8 = byte_at(input, at)
    if c = 116u8:
        if spelled(input, at, ⎕bytes("true")):
            return Scalar(.value ← Value(.boolean ← true), .end ← at + 4)
        return ⊥ Fault(.problem ← Problem.unexpected, .at ← at)
    if c = 102u8:
        if spelled(input, at, ⎕bytes("false")):
            return Scalar(.value ← Value(.boolean ← false), .end ← at + 5)
        return ⊥ Fault(.problem ← Problem.unexpected, .at ← at)
    if c = 110u8:
        if spelled(input, at, ⎕bytes("null")):
            return Scalar(.value ← Value(.null ← true), .end ← at + 4)
        return ⊥ Fault(.problem ← Problem.unexpected, .at ← at)
    number_at(input, at, allow)

※ A number at `at`, and where it ends.
※
※ Whole numbers of up to nineteen digits are read here, a load, a comparison and
※ a multiplication a digit, and are an `integer` or a `large` as they fit.
※ What has a point, an exponent or more digits is handed to `std.string`'s
※ `parse_f64`, from the start again; a hexadecimal number to its `parse_int`.
fn number_at(input: u8⟦⟧, at: u64 ¤idx, allow: Extensions) → Scalar ? Fault in ⎕static:
    let n: u64 ¤idx = #input
    let c: u8 = byte_at(input, at)
    let negative: bool = c = 45u8
    let signed: bool = negative or (c = 43u8 and allows(allow, Extensions.leading_plus))
    let begin: u64 ¤idx = if signed: at + 1 else: at
    let first: u8 = byte_at(input, begin)
    if (first = 78u8 or first = 73u8) and allows(allow, Extensions.special_numbers):
        return special_at(input, at, begin, negative)
    let w: mut u64 = 0u64
    let end: mut u64 ¤idx = begin
    while §digits end < n:
        let d: u8 = ⎕wrap(input⟦end⟧ - 48u8)
        if d ≥ 10u8:
            break §digits
        w ← ⎕wrap(w × 10u64 + ⎕widen(d, ⌜u64⌝))
        end ← end + 1
    let digits: u64 ¤idx = end - begin
    let next: u8 = byte_at(input, end)
    let whole: bool = next ≠ 46u8 and (next | 32u8) ≠ 101u8 and (next | 32u8) ≠ 120u8
    if digits ≥ 1 and digits ≤ 19 and whole:
        if digits > 1 and first = 48u8:
            return ⊥ Fault(.problem ← Problem.malformed_number, .at ← at)
        if ¬negative:
            if w ≤ 0x7FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFu64:
                return Scalar(.value ← Value(.integer ← ⎕narrow(w, ⌜i64⌝) ?? 0i64), .end ← end)
            return Scalar(.value ← Value(.large ← w), .end ← end)
        if w ≤ 0x8000000000000000u64:
            ※ -(w - 1) - 1, which the smallest i64 also fits
            let below: i64 = ⁻1i64 - (⎕narrow(⎕wrap(w - 1u64), ⌜i64⌝) ?? 0i64)
            return Scalar(.value ← Value(.integer ← if w = 0u64: 0i64 else: below), .end ← end)
    if digits = 0 and first ≠ 46u8:
        return ⊥ Fault(.problem ← if begin ≥ n: Problem.truncated else: Problem.unexpected, .at ← begin)
    if digits > 1 and first = 48u8:
        return ⊥ Fault(.problem ← Problem.malformed_number, .at ← at)
    let numbers: string.Allow = number_allow(allow)
    if (next | 32u8) = 120u8 and digits = 1 and first = 48u8 and allows(allow, Extensions.hex_numbers):
        return hex_at(input, at, numbers)
    if whole and digits ≥ 1:
        ※ more than nineteen digits: a large one, if it fits
        ※ too large for that too, it is the f64 nearest it, as any other is
        if ¬negative:
            match string.parse_int(input⟦at…n⟧, ⌜u64⌝, .allow ← numbers):
                string.Parsed(u64)(p):
                    return Scalar(.value ← Value(.large ← p.value), .end ← at + p.end)
                ⊥(why):
                    if why ≠ string.ParseError.out_of_range:
                        return ⊥ Fault(.problem ← Problem.malformed_number, .at ← at)
    match string.parse_f64(input⟦at…n⟧, numbers):
        string.Parsed(f64)(p):
            Scalar(.value ← Value(.number ← p.value), .end ← at + p.end)
        ⊥(why):
            ⊥ Fault(.problem ← if why = string.ParseError.out_of_range: Problem.out_of_range
                             else: Problem.malformed_number, .at ← at)

※ A hexadecimal whole number at `at`: an `integer` where it fits one, a `large`
※ where it is positive and fits that.
fn hex_at(input: u8⟦⟧, at: u64 ¤idx, numbers: string.Allow) → Scalar ? Fault in ⎕static:
    let n: u64 ¤idx = #input
    match string.parse_int(input⟦at…n⟧, ⌜i64⌝, .allow ← numbers):
        string.Parsed(i64)(p):
            return Scalar(.value ← Value(.integer ← p.value), .end ← at + p.end)
        ⊥(why):
            if why ≠ string.ParseError.out_of_range:
                return ⊥ Fault(.problem ← Problem.malformed_number, .at ← at)
    match string.parse_int(input⟦at…n⟧, ⌜u64⌝, .allow ← numbers):
        string.Parsed(u64)(p):
            Scalar(.value ← Value(.large ← p.value), .end ← at + p.end)
        ⊥(why):
            ⊥ Fault(.problem ← if why = string.ParseError.out_of_range: Problem.out_of_range
                             else: Problem.malformed_number, .at ← at)

※ `NaN` or `Infinity` at `begin`, after a sign at `at` where there is one.
fn special_at(input: u8⟦⟧, at: u64 ¤idx, begin: u64 ¤idx, negative: bool) → Scalar ? Fault in ⎕static:
    let sign: u64 = if negative: 0x8000000000000000u64 else: 0u64
    if spelled(input, begin, ⎕bytes("NaN")):
        return Scalar(.value ← Value(.number ← ⎕from_bits(0x7FF8000000000000u64 | sign, ⌜f64⌝)),
                             .end ← begin + 3)
    if spelled(input, begin, ⎕bytes("Infinity")):
        return Scalar(.value ← Value(.number ← ⎕from_bits(0x7FF0000000000000u64 | sign, ⌜f64⌝)),
                             .end ← begin + 8)
    ⊥ Fault(.problem ← Problem.unexpected, .at ← at)

※ -- decoding ------------------------------------------------------------------

※※ A JSON document read out of bytes, into `a`.
※※
※※ \details The bytes are RFC 8259's JSON, and what *allow* adds to it; one value,
※※ with white space around it, or with `Extensions.several` any number of them,
※※ answered as an array.  The text is UTF-8, the strings are checked as such, and
※※ an escape names a code point: a surrogate pair is the one character, and half
※※ of one alone is refused unless `lone_surrogates` says otherwise.  *depth* is
※※ how deeply arrays and objects may be nested, one deeper being refused at its
※※ bracket; what is open is kept on a stack of the reader's own and not the
※※ machine's, so the limit is a policy.  *duplicates* is what is done with a key
※※ an object already had.
※※
※※ Everything the value holds is made in `a`, and lives as long as it does.
※※
※※ \param bytes the document
※※ \param a where the value is made
※※ \param allow what is read besides RFC 8259
※※ \param depth how many arrays and objects deep the document may go
※※ \param duplicates what is done with a key written twice in one object
※※ \return the value
※※ \raises the problem and where it is, the first one found
@[export]
fn decode(bytes: u8⟦⟧, a: &mut arena, allow: Extensions ← Extensions.strict,
          depth: u64 ← 1024u64, duplicates: Duplicates ← Duplicates.keep) → Value ? Error in a:
    let n: u64 ¤idx = #bytes
    if n ≥ 0xFFFFFFFF:
        return ⊥ Error(.problem ← Problem.too_large, .at ← 0, .line ← 1u64, .column ← 1u64)
    let start: u64 ¤idx = if (allows(allow, Extensions.byte_order_mark) and n ≥ 3
                              and bytes⟦0⟧ = 0xEFu8 and bytes⟦1⟧ = 0xBBu8 and bytes⟦2⟧ = 0xBFu8):
        3
    else:
        0
    let lexing: Extensions = (Extensions.comments | Extensions.hash_comments | Extensions.single_quotes
                              | Extensions.whitespace)
    ※ where everything begins, which is done with once the value is read
    let scratch: mut arena = ⎕arena
    let index: [u32] = if allow & lexing ≠ Extensions.strict:
        slowly(bytes, start, allow, &mut scratch)
    else:
        quickly(bytes, start, &mut scratch)
    match document(bytes, index, depth, a, How(.allow ← allow, .duplicates ← duplicates)):
        Value(v):
            v
        ⊥(fault):
            ⊥ failure(bytes, fault)

※ The value the bytes are, the places of everything in them being `index`: one,
※ with nothing after it, or with `several` all of them as an array.
fn document(bytes: u8⟦⟧, index: u32⟦⟧, depth: u64, a: &mut arena, how: How) → Value ? Fault in a:
    let n: u64 ¤idx = #bytes
    if ¬allows(how.allow, Extensions.several):
        let one: Read = tree_at(bytes, index, 0, depth, a, how)?
        let after: u64 ¤idx = place_of(index, one.next)
        if after < n:
            return ⊥ Fault(.problem ← Problem.trailing, .at ← after)
        return one.value
    let all: mut [Value] = [] in a
    let k: mut u64 ¤idx = 0
    while §values place_of(index, k) < n:
        let one: Read = tree_at(bytes, index, k, depth, a, how)?
        all ⧺← [one.value]
        k ← one.next
    Value(.array ← all)

※ -- encoding ------------------------------------------------------------------

※ Sixty-four spaces, the indentation a line takes at most in one move.
let SPACES: u8⟦64⟧ = spaces() in ⎕static

fn spaces() → u8⟦64⟧:
    ⟦32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8,
     32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8,
     32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8,
     32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8, 32u8⟧

※ The hexadecimal digits, small, as Python's `json` writes them.
let HEX: u8⟦16⟧ = hex_digits() in ⎕static

fn hex_digits() → u8⟦16⟧:
    ⟦48u8, 49u8, 50u8, 51u8, 52u8, 53u8, 54u8, 55u8, 56u8, 57u8, 97u8, 98u8, 99u8, 100u8, 101u8, 102u8⟧

※※ Whether every number a value holds is finite, which is what JSON can write.
※※
※※ \param v the value
※※ \return false where a number in it is an infinity or not a number
@[export]
fn finite(v: Value) → bool:
    finite_at(&v)

※ Whether every number `v` names is finite, read where it is.
fn finite_at(v: &Value) → bool:
    match v⌖:
        f64(x):
            ⎕bits(x) & 0x7FF0000000000000u64 ≠ 0x7FF0000000000000u64
        [Value](items):
            foreach &e: Value = items:
                if ¬finite_at(e):
                    return false
            true
        [Member](members):
            foreach &m: Member = members:
                if ¬finite_at(&m⌖.value):
                    return false
            true
        _:
            true

※※ A value written as JSON, UTF-8, into `a`.
※※
※※ \details Compact -- no white space at all -- or with *pretty* one element or
※※ member a line, each *indent* spaces further in than what holds it, a space
※※ after each colon, and an empty array or object as `[]` and `{}`: what Python's
※※ `json.dumps` writes with `separators=(',', ':')` and with `indent=2`.  A string
※※ is written with `"` and `\` escaped, the control characters as `\n`, `\t`,
※※ `\r`, `\b`, `\f` or `\u` and four small hexadecimal digits, and everything else
※※ as it is -- or with *ascii* DEL and every character above it as `\u` escapes,
※※ a surrogate pair for one beyond U+FFFF.  An `integer` and a `large` are written
※※ exactly; a `number` as the shortest text that reads back as it, by
※※ `std.string.numeral_f64` in Python's form -- a point or an exponent always, so
※※ that what is written is decoded as the same value, and what Python's `json`
※※ writes byte for byte.  An infinity or a not-a-number is no JSON, and is
※※ refused unless *special* asks for `Infinity`, `-Infinity` and `NaN`, which
※※ `Extensions.special_numbers` reads.
※※
※※ The bytes are made in `a`, as `decode`'s value is, and go when it goes.
※※
※※ \param v the value
※※ \param a where the bytes are made
※※ \param pretty whether to write it over several lines
※※ \param indent how many spaces each level is further in, where it is pretty
※※ \param ascii whether to write every character above ASCII as an escape
※※ \param special whether a number that is not finite is written rather than refused
※※ \return the bytes
※※ \raises where a number is not finite and *special* is false
@[export]
fn encode(v: Value, a: &mut arena, pretty: bool ← false, indent: u8 ← 2u8, ascii: bool ← false,
          special: bool ← false) → [u8]? in a:
    if ¬special and ¬finite_at(&v):
        return ⊥
    written(&v, [] in a, a, pretty, ⎕widen(indent, ⌜u64⌝), 0u64, ascii)

※ A line's end and `count` spaces, onto `into`.
fn new_line(into: mut [u8] in a, a: &mut arena, count: u64) → [u8] in a:
    into ⧺← ⟦10u8⟧
    let left: mut u64 = count
    while §runs left > 64u64:
        into ⧺← SPACES
        left ← left - 64u64
    into ⧺← SPACES⟦0…⎕unit(left, ⌜idx⌝)⟧
    into

※ `v` written onto `into`, `level` arrays and objects deep.
※
※ **Only arrays and objects are written here, everything else by `leaf_written`,**
※ which is never inlined: this is the one function writing recurses through,
※ once a level, and the room a numeral and a string's blocks take in a frame
※ would otherwise be taken once for every level a document has.
fn written(v: &Value, into: mut [u8] in a, a: &mut arena, pretty: bool, indent: u64, level: u64,
           ascii: bool) → [u8] in a:
    match v⌖:
        [Value](items):
            if #items = 0:
                into ⧺← ⎕bytes("[]")
            else:
                into ⧺← ⟦91u8⟧
                let first: mut bool = true
                foreach &e: Value = items:
                    if ¬first:
                        into ⧺← ⟦44u8⟧
                    first ← false
                    if pretty:
                        into ← new_line(into, a, (level + 1u64) × indent)
                    into ← written(e, into, a, pretty, indent, level + 1u64, ascii)
                if pretty:
                    into ← new_line(into, a, level × indent)
                into ⧺← ⟦93u8⟧
        [Member](members):
            if #members = 0:
                into ⧺← ⎕bytes("{}")
            else:
                into ⧺← ⟦123u8⟧
                let first: mut bool = true
                foreach &m: Member = members:
                    if ¬first:
                        into ⧺← ⟦44u8⟧
                    first ← false
                    if pretty:
                        into ← new_line(into, a, (level + 1u64) × indent)
                    into ← quoted(⎕bytes(m⌖.key), into, a, ascii)
                    into ⧺← ⟦58u8⟧
                    if pretty:
                        into ⧺← ⟦32u8⟧
                    into ← written(&m⌖.value, into, a, pretty, indent, level + 1u64, ascii)
                if pretty:
                    into ← new_line(into, a, level × indent)
                into ⧺← ⟦125u8⟧
        _:
            into ← leaf_written(v, into, a, ascii)
    into

※ A value that is neither an array nor an object written onto `into`.
@[inline(never)]
fn leaf_written(v: &Value, into: mut [u8] in a, a: &mut arena, ascii: bool) → [u8] in a:
    match v⌖:
        void:
            into ⧺← ⎕bytes("null")
        bool(b):
            if b:
                into ⧺← ⎕bytes("true")
            else:
                into ⧺← ⎕bytes("false")
        i64(n):
            let text: string.Numeral = string.numeral_int(n)
            into ⧺← text.text⟦text.from…text.to⟧
        u64(n):
            let text: string.Numeral = string.numeral_int(n)
            into ⧺← text.text⟦text.from…text.to⟧
        f64(x):
            into ← number(x, into, a)
        str(s):
            into ← quoted(⎕bytes(s), into, a, ascii)
        _:
            into ← into
    into

※ A number, as Python's `repr` lays it out: with a point or an exponent always,
※ so that it is read back as a number and not as a whole one.
fn number(x: f64, into: mut [u8] in a, a: &mut arena) → [u8] in a:
    let text: string.Numeral = string.numeral_f64(x, string.Form.python)
    into ⧺← text.text⟦text.from…text.to⟧
    into

※ A string's bytes written between quotes onto `into`, escaped as JSON needs.
※
※ Thirty-two bytes at a time to the first that wants an escape -- a control
※ character, `"`, `\`, and with *ascii* DEL and anything above ASCII, as
※ Python's `ensure_ascii` has it -- and what is before it in one move.
fn quoted(text: u8⟦⟧, into: mut [u8] in a, a: &mut arena, ascii: bool) → [u8] in a:
    let n: u64 ¤idx = #text
    into ⧺← ⟦34u8⟧
    ※ every bit where anything above ASCII is escaped too, and none where not
    let high: u32 = if ascii: 0xFFFFFFFFu32 else: 0u32
    let at: mut u64 ¤idx = 0
    let from: mut u64 ¤idx = 0
    while §runs at < n:
        at ← wanted_at(text, at, high)
        into ⧺← text⟦from…at⟧
        if at < n:
            let c: u8 = text⟦at⟧
            if c < 0x7Fu8:
                into ← escape(c, into, a)
                at ← at + 1
            elif c = 0x7Fu8:
                into ← four(0x7Fu32, into, a)
                at ← at + 1
            else:
                match string.decode_char(text⟦at…n⟧):
                    string.Parsed(char)(p):
                        into ← unicode(⎕ord(p.value), into, a)
                        at ← at + p.end
                    ⊥:
                        ※ text is UTF-8, so this is never reached
                        at ← at + 1
            from ← at
    into ⧺← ⟦34u8⟧
    into

※ The place of the first byte from `from` on that is written as an escape, or
※ the end: thirty-two bytes at a time, the last block copied into room of
※ quotes, which want one.  `high` is every bit where DEL and what is above ASCII
※ want one too.
@[inline]
fn wanted_at(text: u8⟦⟧, from: u64 ¤idx, high: u32) → u64 ¤idx:
    let n: u64 ¤idx = #text
    let at: mut u64 ¤idx = from
    while §blocks true:
        let stops: mut u32 = 0u32
        if at + 32 ≤ n:
            let block: u8⟦32⟧ = text⟦at…at + 32⟧
            stops ← (⎕mask((block < 32u8) ∨ (block = 34u8) ∨ (block = 92u8))
                     | (⎕mask(block ≥ 127u8) & high))
        else:
            let last: mut u8⟦32⟧ = 32 ⍴ 34u8
            last⟦0…n - at⟧ ← text⟦at…n⟧
            stops ← (⎕mask((last < 32u8) ∨ (last = 34u8) ∨ (last = 92u8))
                     | (⎕mask(last ≥ 127u8) & high))
        if stops ≠ 0u32:
            return (at + ⎕unit(⎕widen(⎕trail(stops), ⌜u64⌝), ⌜idx⌝)) ⌊ n
        at ← at + 32
    n

※ An ASCII byte that wants an escape, escaped.
fn escape(c: u8, into: mut [u8] in a, a: &mut arena) → [u8] in a:
    let named: u8 = if c = 34u8 or c = 92u8:
        c
    elif c = 10u8:
        110u8
    elif c = 13u8:
        114u8
    elif c = 9u8:
        116u8
    elif c = 8u8:
        98u8
    elif c = 12u8:
        102u8
    else:
        0u8
    if named ≠ 0u8:
        into ⧺← ⟦92u8, named⟧
        return into
    unicode(⎕widen(c, ⌜u32⌝), into, a)

※ A code point as `\u` escapes: one, or a surrogate pair beyond U+FFFF.
fn unicode(code: u32, into: mut [u8] in a, a: &mut arena) → [u8] in a:
    if code ≥ 0x10000u32:
        let rest: u32 = code - 0x10000u32
        into ← four(0xD800u32 + (rest » 10u32), into, a)
        return four(0xDC00u32 + (rest & 0x3FFu32), into, a)
    four(code, into, a)

※ `\u` and four small hexadecimal digits.
fn four(code: u32, into: mut [u8] in a, a: &mut arena) → [u8] in a:
    into ⧺← ⟦92u8, 117u8, HEX⟦digit(code » 12u32)⟧, HEX⟦digit(code » 8u32)⟧,
             HEX⟦digit(code » 4u32)⟧, HEX⟦digit(code)⟧⟧
    into

※ The low four bits of a number, as a place in `HEX`.
fn digit(n: u32) → u64 ¤idx:
    ⎕unit(⎕widen(n & 15u32, ⌜u64⌝), ⌜idx⌝)

※ -- comparing -----------------------------------------------------------------

※※ Whether two values are one: of the same kind, and the same all the way down.
※※
※※ \details Numbers are the same where their bits are, so an `integer` is not the
※※ `number` of the same value, a not-a-number is itself and the two noughts are
※※ two; text is the same where its bytes are; arrays element by element and
※※ objects member by member, in order, keys and values.
※※
※※ \param x one value
※※ \param y the other
※※ \return whether they are the same
@[export]
fn equal(x: Value, y: Value) → bool:
    match x:
        void:
            match y:
                void: true
                _: false
        bool(b):
            match y:
                bool(c): b = c
                _: false
        i64(n):
            match y:
                i64(m): n = m
                _: false
        u64(n):
            match y:
                u64(m): n = m
                _: false
        f64(p):
            match y:
                f64(q): ⎕bits(p) = ⎕bits(q)
                _: false
        str(s):
            match y:
                str(t): s = t
                _: false
        [Value](l):
            match y:
                [Value](m):
                    if #l ≠ #m:
                        return false
                    let i: mut u64 ¤idx = 0
                    let count: u64 ¤idx = #l
                    while §elements i < count:
                        if ¬equal(l⟦i⟧, m⟦i⟧):
                            return false
                        i ← i + 1
                    true
                _:
                    false
        [Member](l):
            match y:
                [Member](m):
                    if #l ≠ #m:
                        return false
                    let i: mut u64 ¤idx = 0
                    let count: u64 ¤idx = #l
                    while §members i < count:
                        if l⟦i⟧.key ≠ m⟦i⟧.key or ¬equal(l⟦i⟧.value, m⟦i⟧.value):
                            return false
                        i ← i + 1
                    true
                _:
                    false
